## Step 2 — implement
The mechanism, already verified: `Merge3Way` re-marshals every rewritten
both-live row from the modeled `merge.Issue` struct
(`internal/merge/merge.go:133-141`), so a field absent from that struct is
dropped on ALL rewritten rows, not only on true conflicts. `assignee` is
dropped this way today — that is a pre-existing defect you are fixing, not a
regression you introduced.

Model `Assignee` and `ClaimExpiresAt` in `merge.Issue`/`mergeIssue`, merged as a
pair by latest `updated_at` (they change together in a claim), with the lease
cleared when the merged status is not `in_progress` (the assignee is kept).

Your test cases must include the no-conflict pass-through case: an issue changed
on one side in an unrelated field must retain `assignee` and `claim_expires_at`
in the merged output. Conflict-only tests would miss a forgotten copy into
`mergeIssue`'s rebuilt result, which is the exact defect class here.

Your bead does not depend on the storage bead. `internal/merge` models its own
struct and does not import the claim column — if you find yourself needing
`types.Issue.ClaimExpiresAt` to compile, stop and say so in the build report
rather than reaching outside `## Touches`.

## Step 3 — gate and close, in this order
